{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Classification" heading="Decide what runs where">
  <p>
    Node groups match on the facts your nodes already report and hand
    openvox-server the answer over the ENC contract it expects.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl">Groups that match real nodes</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p>
      Build a rule from facts &mdash; <code>os.family = Debian</code>,
      <code>role = web</code> &mdash; and the console tells you how many
      nodes it matches right now. A rule that catches nothing is obvious
      immediately, instead of at the next Puppet run.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "groups-list"}}

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl">Precedence you can read off the page</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p>
      When several groups apply to a node, one number decides which wins.
      Groups are flat and each carries an explicit priority, so working
      out why a node got a class means reading a list, not tracing an
      inheritance tree.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-step-indicator>
  <vox-step>A node asks for its catalog</vox-step>
  <vox-step>openvox-server asks the console what applies</vox-step>
  <vox-step>Matching groups merge by priority</vox-step>
  <vox-step>Classes, parameters and environment come back</vox-step>
</vox-step-indicator>

<vox-callout variant="warning" heading="Rules match on reported facts">
  <p>
    A node that has never checked in has no facts yet, so no fact-based
    rule can match it. Pin it to a group directly if it needs
    classification before its first run.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
